Presidential elections were held in [[Croatia]] on 28 December 2014 and 11 January 2015, the sixth such elections since independence in 1991. Only four candidates contested the elections, the lowest number since [[1997 Croatian presidential election|1997]]. Incumbent president [[Ivo Josipović]], who had been elected as the candidate of the [[Social Democratic Party of Croatia|Social Democratic Party]] in [[2009–2010 Croatian presidential election|2009–2010]], was eligible to seek reelection for a second and final five-year term and ran as an independent. As no candidate received 50% of the vote in the first round in December 2014, a run-off took place in January 2015 between the two candidates with the most votes, Josipović and [[Kolinda Grabar-Kitarović]].<ref name="izborijl">{{cite news|url=http://www.jutarnji.hr/ceka-se-potvrda-iz-vlade-predsjednicki-izbori-odrzat-ce-se-28--prosinca/1237590/|title=ODLUČENO JE Predsjednički izbori održat će se 28. prosinca|date=19 November 2014|work=[[Jutarnji list]]|language=hr|access-date=19 November 2014|archive-date=21 November 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141121192629/http://www.jutarnji.hr/ceka-se-potvrda-iz-vlade-predsjednicki-izbori-odrzat-ce-se-28--prosinca/1237590|url-status=dead}}</ref> Grabar-Kitarović went on to win the elections by a slim margin of 32,509 votes or 1.48%, making her Croatia's first female president.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-30765822|title=Grabar-Kitarovic elected Croatia's first woman president|date=11 January 2015}}</ref>

The elections were the second to have a woman in the run-off, the first having been the [[2005 Croatian presidential election|2005 elections]], and also featured the youngest candidate to run in a presidential contest, [[Ivan Vilibor Sinčić]], aged 24. The election of Grabar-Kitarović was the first victory for the [[Croatian Democratic Union]] (HDZ) in a presidential election since the death of [[Franjo Tuđman]] in December 1999, making her the first right-wing President of Croatia in 15 years. The defeat of Josipović marked the first time that an incumbent Croatian President had failed to win reelection for a second five-year term, with both his predecessors [[Franjo Tuđman]] and [[Stjepan Mesić]] serving two terms. The number of votes (1,114,900) received by Grabar-Kitarović in the second round was the lowest number of votes received by an elected Croatian President until that point in time (later being surpassed by the number of votes for [[Zoran Milanović]] in the [[2019-20 Croatian presidential election|2019-20 election]]). Grabar-Kitarović was sworn in as the fourth President of Croatia on 15 February 2015, becoming the youngest person to take office as President of the Republic of Croatia, aged 46 years and 295 days.

==Background and rules==
In mid-October 2014 the SDP-led government proposed adopting a new electoral law by February 2015. SDP's parliamentary speaker Josip Leko stated that the party's position in consultation with the [[Venice Commission]] was that the electoral law should not be changed within a year prior to an election.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://hr.n1info.com/a5427/Vijesti/Izmjene-izbornog-zakona-do-veljace.html|title=Izmjene izbornog zakona do veljače?|date=21 October 2014|work=N1|language=hr|access-date=3 January 2015|archive-date=3 January 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150103235827/http://hr.n1info.com/a5427/Vijesti/Izmjene-izbornog-zakona-do-veljace.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> However, the new ''Law on the Election of the President of the Republic of Croatia'' was subsequently voted in by the SDP-led parliamentary majority on 24 October.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.jutarnji.hr/sdp-izmijenio-zakon-o-izboru-predsjednika-rh--ljutiti-hdz-ovci-napustili-sabor/1230571/|title=SDP izmijenio Zakon o izboru predsjednika, ljutiti HDZ-ovci napustili Sabor|date=24 October 2014|work=[[Jutarnji list]]|language=hr|access-date=3 January 2015|archive-date=4 January 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150104000553/http://www.jutarnji.hr/sdp-izmijenio-zakon-o-izboru-predsjednika-rh--ljutiti-hdz-ovci-napustili-sabor/1230571/|url-status=dead}}</ref> The opposition Croatian Democratic Union (HDZ) walked out on the vote criticizing the timing, while most of the parliament's minority representatives voted against the law due to a lack of consultation of parliamentary groups.<ref name="Glas Istre">{{cite news|url=http://www.glasistre.hr/vijesti/hrvatska/sabor-izmijenio-zakon-o-izboru-predsjednika-rh-478031|title=Sabor izmijenio Zakon o izboru predsjednika RH|date=24 October 2014|work=[[Glas Istre]]|language=hr|access-date=4 January 2015}}</ref> The SDP's [[Peđa Grbin]], head of the parliament's constitutional committee jeered the opposition: "I understand why my colleagues from HDZ are opposed, since they won't have to wait until midnight to find that they've also lost these elections" - ostensibly in reference to a part of the law which shortened the electoral silence from midnight on election day to the closing of the polls.<ref name="Glas Istre"/>

One of the more significant changes to the law involved limiting voting abroad to consular offices. This had the effect of greatly reducing the number of polling stations in neighbouring [[Bosnia and Herzegovina]]: from 124 in the 2009-10 presidential elections to 15 in the current election.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.dnevnik.ba/novosti/bih/hns-tra%C5%BEi-vi%C5%A1e-bira%C4%8Dkih-mjesta-u-bih-za-hrvatske-predsjedni%C4%8Dke-izbore|title=HNS traži više biračkih mjesta u BiH za hrvatske predsjedničke izbore|date=28 November 2014|work=Dnevnik.ba|language=hr|access-date=3 January 2015|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150104003257/http://www.dnevnik.ba/novosti/bih/hns-tra%C5%BEi-vi%C5%A1e-bira%C4%8Dkih-mjesta-u-bih-za-hrvatske-predsjedni%C4%8Dke-izbore|archive-date=4 January 2015}}</ref> The [[Croatian People's Assembly]], a grouping of Croat parties in Bosnia and Herzegovina, protested at the reduction. Overall the number of polling stations abroad was reduced from 250 to 90.

On 20 November Croatian prime minister Zoran Milanović called presidential elections to take place on 28 December.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.izbori.hr/izbori/ws.nsf/C17D82D38F7B097DC1257D970058DBA1/$FILE/Odluka_Vlade_RH_o_raspisivanju_izbora_za_predsjednika_RH_28.12.2014..pdf|title=Odluka Vlade Republike Hrvatske o raspisivanju izbora za predsjednika Republike Hrvatske|date=20 November 2014|work= Državno izborno povjerenstvo|access-date=4 January 2015}}</ref>

Opinions polls in late 2014 showed the Croatian public with high disapproval ratings of the country's direction and the government: 82% and 79% respectively.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://dnevnik.hr/vijesti/hrvatska/dnevnik-novatv-crobarometar-prosinac-2014-hdz-i-sdp-rasli-orah-pao---365410.html|title=Ekskluzivno u Dnevniku Nove TV: HDZ uvjerljivo prvi|date=18 December 2014|work= dnevnik.hr|access-date=5 January 2015}}</ref>

==Campaign before the official start==
Kolinda Grabar-Kitarović was first discussed as the Croatian Democratic Union's candidate in May 2014 after the party emerged with the most votes and seats nationally in the [[2014 European Parliament election in Croatia|European Parliament elections]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://dalje.com/en-croatia/karamarko--grabar-kitarovic-hdzs-most-likely-presidential-candidate/510183|title=Karamarko: Grabar Kitarovic HDZ's most likely presidential candidate|date=27 May 2014|work=Dalje.com|access-date=4 January 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150104090017/http://dalje.com/en-croatia/karamarko--grabar-kitarovic-hdzs-most-likely-presidential-candidate/510183|archive-date=4 January 2015|url-status=dead|df=dmy-all}}</ref> Her candidature was confirmed by party leader [[Tomislav Karamarko]] on 12 June.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://dalje.com/en-croatia/kolinda-grabar-kitarovic-is-hdzs-presidential-candidate/511823|title=Kolinda Grabar Kitarovic is HDZ's presidential candidate|date=12 June 2014|work=Dalje.com|access-date=4 January 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150104090019/http://dalje.com/en-croatia/kolinda-grabar-kitarovic-is-hdzs-presidential-candidate/511823|archive-date=4 January 2015|url-status=dead|df=dmy-all}}</ref>

In October Josipović had to deal with the fallout from an article written by his chief analyst Dejan Jović claiming that the 1991 Croatian independence referendum was "quite illiberal and was not held in free and honest circumstances".<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.24sata.hr/politika/josipovic-razrijesio-savjetnika-radi-stava-o-referendumu-1991-387002|title=Josipović razriješio savjetnika zbog stava o referendumu '91.|date=2 October 2014|work= 24sata.hr|access-date=4 January 2015}}</ref> Josipović subsequently dismissed Jović.

==Candidates==
Candidates seeking nomination first had to submit a minimum of 10,000 citizens' signatures to the State Electoral Commission (''Državno izborno povjerenstvo'' or DIP) in order for their candidacy to be formally accepted. The signatures had to be collected in a period of twelve days, from 25 November to 6 December. The deadline for submissions was midnight, 6 December, with most candidates submitting a much larger number of signatures as a show of support. Following the submissions, DIP had two days to check the validity of signatures and announced a final list of candidates by midnight on 8 December. On 9 December the campaign officially started, and lasted for 18 days until 26 December, celebrated in Croatia as [[Saint Stephen's Day]].

The first to submit his signatures was Milan Kujundžić who handed them in on 5 December. Incumbent president Ivo Josipović submitted his 203,875 signatures along with prime minister Zoran Milanović and other members of his cabinet on the day of the deadline.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.24sata.hr/politika/predaja-kandidatura-apic-je-odustao-a-grubisic-razmislja-397079|title=I Milanović pomogao dostaviti Josipovićevih 203.000 potpisa|date=6 December 2014|work= 24sata.hr|language=hr|access-date=4 January 2015}}</ref> Kolinda Grabar-Kitarović and Ivan Sinčić both also submitted the required signatures later that day.

