===Withdrawn candidate bids===
* [[Anto Đapić]] withdrew on 5 December, endorsing Kolinda Grabar-Kitarović.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.jutarnji.hr/anto-dapic-odustao-od-kandidature--procijenio-sam-da-nema-sanse--podrsku-dajem-kolindi-/1247993/|title=ANTO ĐAPIĆ DAJE PODRŠKU KOLINDI 'Odustajem od kandidature, to bi samo zbunilo desničare'|date=5 December 2014|work=[[Jutarnji list]]|language=hr|access-date=6 December 2014|archive-date=8 December 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141208210107/http://www.jutarnji.hr/anto-dapic-odustao-od-kandidature--procijenio-sam-da-nema-sanse--podrsku-dajem-kolindi-/1247993/|url-status=dead}}</ref>
* [[Ivan Grubišić]] withdrew on 6 December, endorsing Ivan Rude.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.index.hr/vijesti/clanak/i-don-ivan-grubisic-odustao-od-predsjednicke-kandidature-ne-zelim-sudjelovati-u-ovakvoj-kampanji/788452.aspx|title = I don Ivan Grubišić odustao od predsjedničke kandidature: "Ne želim sudjelovati u ovakvoj kampanji"}}</ref>
* Ivan Rude, lawyer who handled pre-bankruptcy settlements at several large companies and candidate of the non-parliamentary Voice of Reason party, he ran on left-wing populist and worker's rights platform. Withdrew on 6 December after failing to collect 10,000 signatures required to formally submit his bid.<ref name="rudeout">{{cite news|url=http://www.vecernji.hr/hrvatska/kandidat-ivan-rude-ipak-nije-uspio-prikupiti-10000-potpisa-977688|title=Kandidat Ivan Rude ipak nije uspio prikupiti 10.000 potpisa|date=6 December 2014|work=Večernji list|language=hr|access-date=6 December 2014}}</ref>

===Failed candidacies===
* Ivan Bavčević submitted 7,600 signatures to the electoral commission on December 6 and was rejected.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.tportal.hr/vijesti/predsjednicki-izbori-2014/361793/Bavcevic-objasnio-kako-je-dosao-do-7-600-potpisa.html|title = Bavčević objasnio kako je došao do 7.600 potpisa}}</ref>
* Ratko Dobrović submitted no signatures to the electoral commission claiming that they are "blank signatures".
* Slobodan Midžić not submitted signatures on the prescribed form. Instead he offered a CD on which, as he claims, is about 500 thousand signatures. He is from [[Velika Kladuša]] ([[Bosnia and Herzegovina|BiH]]).<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.index.hr/vijesti/clanak/ovo-su-predsjednicki-kandidati-koji-su-ostali-u-utrci-za-pantovcak/788482.aspx|title=Ovo su predsjednički kandidati koji su ostali u utrci za Pantovčak}}</ref> Midžić had also failed to nominate himself for [[2009–10 Croatian presidential election|2009 presidential election]].
* Ivica Dukić submitted only 800 signatures because "in Split and Zagreb it is raining and people are suspicious".{{citation needed|date=March 2019}}
* Vesna Balenović,<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.womeninadria.com/zvizdacica-vesna-balenovic-na-celu-drzave/|title = "Zviždačica" Vesna Balenović kao predsjednica RH|date = 10 August 2014}}</ref> supported [[Milan Kujundžić]]
* Tomislav Šutalo,<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.valpovstina.info/index.php/vijesti/1424-predstavio-se-tomislav-sutalo-kandidat-za-predsjednika-republike|title = Predstavio se Tomislav Šutalo, kandidat za predsjednika Republike}}</ref> businessman from Valpovo
* Ivan Valek,<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.24sata.hr/politika/svi-zele-na-pantovcak-u-utrci-mormon-zvizdacica-aktivist-395460|title=Svi žele na Pantovčak: U utrci mormon, zviždačica, aktivist}}</ref> architect and member of The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints
* Iva Anzulović,<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://savjest.com/glas_savjesti/glas.php?glas=141&iva-anzulovic-se-kandidirala-za-predsjednicu-rh|title = Iva Anzulović se kandidirala za predsjednicu RH, glas savjesti - dnevni komentar, autor: Iva Anzulović}}</ref> reporter. She said that "she is apolitical and always a reporter"
* Tomislav Opačak,<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.sbplus.hr/kolumne/manjinas/tomislav_opacak_zeli_biti_predsjednik_svih_hrvata.aspx|title = Sbplus.hr, Slavonski Brod : Kolumne : Manjinaš : Tomislav Opačak želi biti predsjednik svih Hrvata}}</ref> businessman from Slavonski Brod. At the time, he was due to serve a prison sentence for a traffic violation. He said that "there never was a great president who did not serve prison time", mentioning [[Josip Broz Tito]] and [[Franjo Tuđman]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://slobodnadalmacija.hr/novosti/hrvatska/clanak/id/254770/idem-u-zatvor-i-bit-cu-veliki-predsjednik--taj-su-put-prosli-tito-i-tuman|title='Idem u zatvor i bit ću veliki predsjednik - taj su put prošli Tito i Tuđman'|date=26 November 2014|work=[[Slobodna Dalmacija]]|language=hr|access-date=20 March 2019}}</ref>

==Campaign==
During the course of the first-round campaign the head of the president's War Veterans Council Vlado Marić gave his resignation in protest of Josipović. His resignation cited a lack of an "appropriate reaction to multiple attempts to show either <nowiki>[Josipović]</nowiki> or his close associates, personally or through the War Veterans Council, of the difficulty and seriousness of the problems affecting Croatian veterans".<ref>{{cite web| url = http://dnevnik.hr/vijesti/hrvatska/vlado-maric-dao-ostavku-na-mjesto-predsjednika-josipoviceva-savjeta-za-ratne-veterane---366035.html| title = Vlado Marić dao ostavku na mjesto predsjednika Josipovićeva Savjeta za ratne veterane | date = 23 December 2014 | work = Dnevnik.hr | language = hr}}</ref>

Incumbent president Ivo Josipović addressed his supporters on the night of the first round of elections. During his speech he stated "Too much blood has been spilled for Croatia and too many defenders died that we would give Croatia up to those who don't deserve it" in reference to the opposition challenger Grabar-Kitarović.<ref>{{cite web| url = http://www.vecernji.hr/predsjednicki-izbori2014/sokirali-ih-rezultati-u-josipovicevom-stozeru-zavladao-muk-981496| title = Josipović: Previše je krvi proliveno da bi Hrvatsku predali onima koji je ne zaslužuju | date = December 28, 2014 | work = [[Večernji list]] | language = hr}}</ref> The Croatian Democratic Union criticized the president's rhetoric as "dirty and aggressive" and stressed that while Josipović "speaks of blood", their candidate offers "peace, unity and optimism".<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.jutarnji.hr/paljba-iz-hdz-a--josipovic-govori-o-krvi--a-kolinda-nudi-mir--zajednistvo-i-optimizam-/1266915/ | title = PALJBA IZ HDZ-a 'Josipović govori o krvi, a Kolinda nudi mir, zajedništvo i optimizam' | date = January 2, 2015 | work = [[Jutarnji list]] | language = hr | access-date = January 3, 2015 | archive-date = January 4, 2015 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20150104015510/http://www.jutarnji.hr/paljba-iz-hdz-a--josipovic-govori-o-krvi--a-kolinda-nudi-mir--zajednistvo-i-optimizam-/1266915/ | url-status = dead }}</ref>

After the first round, third place candidate Ivan Sinčić was asked which of the two candidates he would support in the second round to which he replied that he could not support either due to moral reasons. Sinčić's Živi zid told his supporters to spoil their ballots in the second round by entering Sinčić's name on the ballot.<ref>{{cite web| url = http://www.vecernji.hr/predsjednicki-izbori2014/pozivamo-vas-da-na-vas-glasacki-listic-dopisete-broj-3-i-zaokruzite-ivana-sincica-981589| title = Preporučili svojim biračima za 2. krug: Dopišite broj 3 i zaokružite ime Ivana Sinčića! | date = December 29, 2014 | work = [[Večernji list]] | language = hr}}</ref> Vice-president of the SDP government [[Milanka Opačić]] called Sinčić a "colourful lie" in an interview from the Josipović campaign headquarters on the night of the first-round elections.<ref>{{cite web| url = http://www.vecernji.hr/predsjednicki-izbori2014/milanka-opacic-gospodin-sincic-je-jedna-sarena-laza-981505| title = Iz Sinčićeva stožera uzvratili Opačić: Vaša politika je politika propasti Hrvatske! | date = 28 December 2014 | work = [[Večernji list]] | language = hr}}</ref> In response Sinčić's campaign manager Dušan Cvetanović called SDP and the government "a colourful lie which ever fewer people have faith in". SDP vice-president and director of operations for Josipović's campaign [[Željka Antunović]] also said that "Sinčić acted like an anarchist and didn't offer solutions".<ref>{{cite web| url = http://www.tportal.hr/vijesti/predsjednicki-izbori-2014/364077/Kod-Josipovica-zavladao-muk-nadaju-se-Sincicevim-glasovima.html| title = Autogol Milanke Opačić: Sinčić je jedna šarena laža | date = 28 December 2014 | work = tportal.hr | language = hr}}</ref> Nevertheless, the following day Josipović reached out to Sinčić's voters: "I hope that those voters who were for Sinčić recognize that this is a field where we're playing together".<ref>{{cite web| url = http://www.vecernji.hr/hrvatska/josipovic-sincic-i-ja-zajedno-igramo-na-istom-terenu-981688| title = Josipović: Sinčić i ja zajedno igramo na istom terenu | date = 29 December 2014 | work = [[Večernji list]] | language = hr}}</ref> Sinčić continued his work with Živi zid after the first round. He was arrested during an attempt to prevent an eviction in Zagreb on 8 January.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://dalje.com/en-croatia/would-be-president-sincic-arrested-during-eviction/532889 | title = Would-be president Sincic arrested during eviction | date = 8 January 2015 | work = Dalje.com | access-date = 9 January 2015 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20150110013326/http://dalje.com/en-croatia/would-be-president-sincic-arrested-during-eviction/532889 | archive-date = 10 January 2015 | url-status = dead | df = dmy-all }}</ref>

Fourth placed candidate Milan Kujundžić implicitly endorsed Grabar-Kitarović during his concession speech on the first-round election night stating that "Croatia will get a new president".<ref>{{cite web| url =http://www.tportal.hr/vijesti/predsjednicki-izbori-2014/364090/Kujundzic-Sjeme-nove-Hrvatske-je-baceno.html| title = Kujundžić: Sjeme nove Hrvatske je bačeno | date = 28 December 2014 | work = tportal.hr | language = hr}}</ref> The Croatian Party of Rights which had backed Kujundžić subsequently endorsed Grabar-Kitarović.

Grabar-Kitarović entered the second round as only the second woman to do so after [[Jadranka Kosor]] in 2005, and attempting to be the first to win the presidency. In prime minister Milanović's new-year interview with [[RTL Televizija|RTL]] he referred to her as a "prima ballerina" in the previous HDZ government when she served as minister.<ref>{{cite web| url =http://www.rtl.hr/vijesti/novosti/1466120/tko-je-koliko-letio-vladinim-zrakoplovom/| title = Tko je koliko letio Vladinim zrakoplovom? | date = 4 January 2015 | work = RTL | language = hr}}</ref> In the interview Milanović also referred to Croatian Catholic bishops as "the most backward in Europe" and referred to Orthodox Christmas as the only non-working day in the coming weeks, despite Ephipany being celebrated as a national non-working holiday on 6 January and Orthodox Christmas being an optional non-working holiday reserved for those Orthodox observing it.<ref>{{cite web| url =http://www.rtl.hr/vijesti/novosti/1464998/milanovic-glogoski-me-je-molio-da-ne-otkrijem-da-je-bio-kod-mene/| title = Milanović: Glogoški me je molio da ne otkrijem da je bio kod mene | date = 3 January 2015 | work = RTL | language = hr}}</ref>

Josipović continued with some events in his presidential role during the campaign. On 6 January he was to attend the Serbian National Council's Christmas party. Traditionally, the event is attended by the president although Josipović had last been in 2012 after a falling-out with the head of the council [[Milorad Pupovac]].<ref>{{cite web| url =http://www.index.hr/vijesti/clanak/sve-za-glasove-nakon-tri-godine-josipovic-ide-na-pupovcev-domjenak/793439.aspx| title = Sve za glasove: Nakon tri godine Josipović ide na Pupovčev domjenak | date = 5 January 2015 | work = Index.hr | language = hr}}</ref><ref>{{cite web| url =http://www.vecernji.hr/hrvatska/karamarko-nece-pupovcu-u-goste-982489| title = Karamarko neće Pupovcu u goste | date = 5 January 2015 | work = Večernji list | language = hr}}</ref>

